+## 3. Full-Spectrum Evaluations
+
+### R1 · OpenAI GPT-5.6 family (Sol / Terra / Luna) + ChatGPT Work — `adapt_cleanroom`
+
+**What shipped (2026-07-09):** three-tier family — Sol (workhorse), Terra (mid), Luna
+(budget). Vendor claims Sol is 54% more token-efficient on coding tasks and the family is
+the "strongest cybersecurity model yet" (threat modeling, code review/patching, blue
+teaming). ChatGPT Work ships as an enterprise clerical companion (docs, sheets, decks).
+US administration pressure on the rollout was reported before launch.
+
+- **Pros:** tier taxonomy (workhorse/mid/budget) matches real routing economics; explicit
+  defensive-cyber positioning gives enterprises a vocabulary to scope allowed use;
+  token-efficiency as a headline metric validates cost-per-task (not cost-per-token) as
+  the KPI that matters — the same KPI SupremeAI's cadence loops should hold.
+- **Cons:** efficiency numbers are vendor-reported on vendor-chosen tasks; the cyber
+  positioning arrives with government-restriction history, meaning availability and
+  policy posture can change under non-technical pressure; ChatGPT Work is a closed
+  runtime that locks clerical workflow data into one vendor surface.
+- **Risks:** single-vendor dependency for "work" workloads; cyber-capable models behind
+  policy shifts; efficiency claims decay across model generations (see R4: the same lab
+  pattern — new tier, more tokens — appears in competitor lines too).
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** adopting a model family because of a launch-cycle headline;
+  routing all tasks to the "flagship" tier by default.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** SupremeAI MUST NOT wire any single vendor family as a default
+  dependency, and MUST NOT enable cyber-offense-adjacent prompts in any agent surface.
+  No blind copy of the "orders of magnitude" marketing (it did not survive independent
+  cost-per-task scrutiny in third-party studies, see R11).
+- **SupremeAI adoption path:** clean-room implement the *pattern* — a tier router with
+  explicit per-request token budgets (Issue P-01) — while keeping providers swappable.
+
+### R2 · OpenAI GPT-6 + Intelligent UI; "dot" always-on agent — `quarantine_eval`
+
+**What shipped (2026-10-07 per Evertune tracker; dot hands-on 2026-10-01):** a flagship
+generation plus an always-on personal agent that keeps context between conversations and
+reports its own limits. This is the persistent-agent-runtime pattern going consumer.
+
+- **Pros:** demonstrates that persistent memory + long-horizon scheduling is now a
+  product baseline, validating SupremeAI's memory-sidecar/episodic architecture bets;
+  "reports its own limits" is a UX worth clean-rooming for agent honesty.
+- **Cons:** closed runtime; the always-on pattern multiplies attack surface (session
+  continuity = persistent credential exposure); third-party hands-on notes context
+  continuity changes failure modes (stale-context drift), not just wins.
+- **Risks:** always-on agents make silent cost bleed and silent scope creep the default;
+  consumer-grade persistence patterns are NOT enterprise governance patterns.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** "assistant that never resets" without per-session budget
+  ledgers; treating vendor UI conventions as a spec.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no SupremeAI agent may run always-on without (a) a token-budget
+  ledger per slot, and (b) a kill-switch reachable by Admin. No persistence of user
+  context across tenants, ever.
+- **SupremeAI adoption path:** quarantine until Issues P-11 (per-slot budget caps) and
+  P-05 (breach-drill harness) land; then eval in an isolated lane only.
