+### R4 · Anthropic Claude Opus 4.6 / Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 — `adapt_cleanroom`
+
+**What shipped:** Opus 4.6 at 80.8% SWE-bench Verified (reference frontier, per Morph
+roundup cited in DeepSeek V4 report); Opus 5.5 ranked most complete in a 21-run reviewed
+Harbor-migration study; Fable 5.1 spent +69% output tokens vs Fable 5 with 2.6x diff
+churn and broader system-wide refactors (Trilogy matched-pilot, 2026-09-03). Financials:
+$80.7B H1-2026 net loss (mostly accounting), IPO-window disclosure of cyber and
+self-replication risk factors (Reuters/WSJ, 2026-10-20/21).
+
+- **Pros:** strongest reviewed-implementation record among frontier agents — the blind
+  study is exactly the evidence culture SupremeAI's PR Guardian should encode; the
+  Fable 5.1 churn study is a gift: it proves model bumps need regression gates on
+  *behavior* (tokens, churn), not just benchmark scores.
+- **Cons:** churn regression means "newest = better" is false in measurable ways;
+  vendor financial instability (valuation step-down talk) is a supply-risk signal for
+  pricing/availability, not a quality signal — keep them separate.
+- **Risks:** anchoring the ecosystem to one vendor's agent harness; diff churn at 2.6x
+  multiplies review load — deadly for a bot-supervised review pipeline.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** auto-upgrading pinned models on release day; letting an
+  agent run "system-wide refactors" without scope fences.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no agent may refactor beyond the issue's declared scope paths —
+  the Fable 5.1 churn data is the empirical proof behind our Boundary invariant. And no
+  procurement/strategy decisions keyed to IPO rumors.
+- **SupremeAI adoption path:** clean-room the *measurement* (Issue P-03) and the
+  reviewed-implementation judging method (Issue P-10), not the models themselves.
